// D1 round 5 / H8 — NAMING THE SCRUTINEE IS INERT. The twin of
// fail/bc_d1r5_h8_match_tmp_scrutinee, differing in exactly one line: the
// scrutinee is bound to `w` before the match instead of being a temporary.
//
// This spelling refuses without the new rule: the loan is held by `w`, which
// the pattern bindings inherit through bc_hop_roots + propagate_pat_loans.
// Fire-count evidence, taken with a temporary counter inside
// retain_temp_scrut_loan and then removed: it fires ONCE on the temporary twin
// and ZERO times on this one. That is what makes the pair an INVARIANT PIN — a
// program's verdict must not depend on whether an intermediate value was given
// a name — and it is why both halves are registered: with the H8 rule reverted
// only the temporary half reds, and a lone red looks like a new defect rather
// than a broken invariant.
//
// ⚠ AND IT IS THE CORPUS'S ONLY SINGLE-RULE DISCRIMINATOR FOR H5b. Measured by
// control revert with a proven restore between the legs:
//
//   fixture                        fixed   H5b-revert   H8-revert
//   this one                       refuse   ADMIT        refuse
//   …h8_match_tmp_scrutinee        refuse   ADMIT        ADMIT
//   …h8_matchexpr_tmp              refuse   ADMIT        ADMIT
//
// H5b (each_pat_binding's eleven un-handled pattern kinds, on the CHECKER side)
// had NO discriminating test before this: in every previously-tried shape the
// SOURCE PLACE's own loan refused independently, so reverting H5b alone
// produced zero reds — mutual redundancy, invisible to one-at-a-time controls.
// Here the whole extraction is intra-procedural, so no flow summary is
// involved and the Struct arm of the checker's pattern walk is the only thing
// that can hand `w`'s loan to `got`. Starve it and this program compiles.
package bc_d1r5_h8_match_named_twin;
#[borrow_carrying]
struct B { p: &i64 }
struct C { v: i64 }
struct Wrap { b: B }
impl C {
    fn mk_wrap(&self) -> Wrap { return Wrap { b: B { p: &self.v } }; }
    fn bump(&mut self) { self.v = self.v + 1i64; }
}
fn main() -> i64 {
    let z: i64 = 7i64;
    let mut c: C = C { v: 1i64 };
    let mut inner: B = B { p: &z };
    let w: Wrap = c.mk_wrap();
    match w { Wrap { b: got } => { inner = got; } }
    c.bump();
    return *inner.p;
}
